# CQ-21  What is the electrical topology path between two assets?
# Group F -- SHIFT is EXPECTED TO FAIL this. Failure here is the correct
# result; the design rule for the CQ set requires questions SHIFT loses.
#
# Parameters (method note 5): the two endpoint assets. No threshold applies.
#
# MISSING TERM (the point of this query). SHIFT has no conducting-equipment
# model. It declares shift:Node (a location/aggregation point) and
# shift:connectedTo (domain Asset, range Asset), but:
#   - there is no Terminal / ConnectivityNode class, so an asset has no
#     electrical endpoints to path through;
#   - there is no ACLineSegment / Switch / Busbar or any branch class, so
#     nothing represents the conductor BETWEEN two points;
#   - shift:connectedTo carries no electrical semantics (no impedance, no
#     phase, no direction) and is asserted on no individual in the ABox.
# The nearest CIM equivalents are cim:ConductingEquipment, cim:Terminal,
# cim:ConnectivityNode and cim:ACLineSegment. This is exactly why the paper
# assesses CIM at alignment level rather than query level.
#
# The query below is the closest SHIFT admits -- transitive closure over
# shift:connectedTo -- and returns nothing.
PREFIX shift: <https://w3id.org/shift/core#>
PREFIX xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
SELECT ?fromAsset ?toAsset
WHERE {
  # -- parameters --------------------------------------------------------
  # Two assets that genuinely exist and are genuinely co-located: both are
  # owned by actor A000 and both sit at node Inishmore. A physical path between
  # them certainly exists; SHIFT simply cannot express it.
  VALUES ?FROM_ASSET { <https://w3id.org/shift/kg/asset/LOAD-000> }
  VALUES ?TO_ASSET   { <https://w3id.org/shift/kg/asset/METER-000> }
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  BIND(?FROM_ASSET AS ?fromAsset)
  BIND(?TO_ASSET   AS ?toAsset)
  ?FROM_ASSET shift:connectedTo+ ?TO_ASSET .
}
